 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per Gaussian per ray, assuming the 2D integrated density $f_0(p)$ can be computed analytically or via a precomputed projection. This is significantly faster than discrete ray marching which is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of samples.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory for the integration step.
-- **Strengths:** Exact analytical solution eliminates discretization error. Extremely fast evaluation enables real-time rendering of dense Gaussian fields (e.g., 3D Gaussian Splatting).
-- **Limitations:** Only applicable when the density field is explicitly modeled as a Gaussian (or a function with a known cumulative distribution function that allows this substitution). It does not handle arbitrary density fields or non-linear transfer functions directly without approximation.
-- **When to use:** When rendering scenes represented by 3D Gaussians (e.g., 3DGS) and high performance is required.
-- **When not to use:** For arbitrary voxel data or non-Gaussian primitives where the integral cannot be simplified analytically.
